// @check
// @error no field 'r' on Sq
// A PARENTHESISED WRITE RETIRES THE NARROWING IT WRITES THROUGH, and this is the SILENT
// half of silent-class-inventory D352 — the half its filed row, a loud check reject, did
// not have.
//
// `placeKeyOf` is the CHECKER's narrowing key and it read `P.nodes[ix]` raw, so `(t).v`
// keyed `""`. Fifteen callers read that key and only two are the member READ; this one is
// the assignment. With no key the write retired no narrowing, the next `t.v.r` read the
// stale `Circle`, and the program was `vl check` rc 0 and a `wasm trap: cast failure` at
// run time. Delete the parens on the WRITE and the same program is this file's diagnostic
// — which is what says the two spellings were a dialect apart rather than a gap.
//
// THE DIAGNOSTIC MOVED WITH D1736's re-ruling and the retirement is what it now pins.
// The write itself is legal — a place accepts its DECLARED type, so `Shape` takes a `Sq` —
// and it is the retired narrowing that makes the `t.v.r` below refuse. Lose the key again
// and this program compiles and traps, exactly as before.
//
// D1847 SHARPENED IT ONE STEP FURTHER: the write does not only retire, it RE-NARROWS the path
// to the member it wrote, so the read names `Sq` rather than the whole union. That is the same
// pin at a finer grain — the key is still what makes it fire.
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
type Holder = { v: Shape }

function f(t: Holder) {
  if t.v is Circle {
    (t).v = { s: 3 }
    print(t.v.r)
  } else {
    print(0)
  }
}
f({ v: { r: 7 } })
